{"id":"refine_20260929121346370","summary":"Updated the global memory for task-b2cb1132 with the correct 11×7 result and the boundary-inclusive projection detail.","rationale":"The fresh instance was solved after the first output missed the far-edge cell on rows projected from the right border bar. The corrected output confirms that projected rays fill every interior cell up to the opposite edge, while preserving the bar cell.","expectedOutcome":"Future instances should include both far-edge interior cells and the marker-to-bar details; validate by checking ray endpoints, marker recoloring, bar-offset alignment, and preservation of unrelated cells.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"continual_arc_task_b2cb1132_project_marker_rays_between_border_bars","title":"Continual-ARC task-b2cb1132: project marker rays between border bars","content":"For Continual-ARC task-b2cb1132, identify the uniform background, two equal-length special-color bars on opposite borders, and sparse marker cells. Preserve both bars and unrelated cells. Recolor each marker to 4. Draw from each marker a straight ray in its original marker color toward its aligned border bar, filling only the cells between marker and bar. Also project marker positions along their bar span to the opposite bar: at the corresponding offset, draw an inward ray from that opposite bar in the marker color. Opposite-bar rows match the same offset from the top/end of each bar. Rays fill every interior cell between the bar and the far edge, including the last interior column when projecting from a left-edge bar or column 0 when projecting from a right-edge bar; preserve only the bar cell itself. Do not extend a marker-to-own-bar ray past the marker.\n\nValidated 11x7 instance: background 5; color-0 bars at the left edge, rows 4–6, and right edge, rows 0–2; color-7 markers at (4,3) and (5,3). The correct output fills rows 0–1, columns 0–5 with 7; fills rows 4–5, columns 1–2 with 7; changes the markers t